      score: -1,
      verdict: "WEAKENING",
      tag: "Two legs broke on the same day: July GDP printed 0.0% m/m and the oil premium that was the other half of the case is deflating",
      drivers: "TE re-verified: the overnight rate is 2.25%, held at the September decision, with TE recording that the Governing Council flagged stronger upside inflation risks, noted a broad Q2 recovery, pointed to risks from the continuing war and renewed US tariffs on Canadian exports, but also said underlying inflation has so far maintained its target level despite the energy shock. Statistics Canada reported real GDP essentially unchanged in July at 0.0% m/m after three consecutive months of expansion, with manufacturing -0.9% and mining, quarrying and oil and gas extraction -0.5%, offset by construction +1.3% and utilities +1.7%; the initial August estimate is +0.2% and will be revised. Macklem has said the Bank needs to look beyond the initial shock of higher oil prices, with his conditional being that inflation edges up if oil stays near 100 dollars - WTI is 89.627 and Middle East exports are at 98% of pre-war levels. The Bank cut its 2026 GDP projection to 0.7% from 1.2% in April. October 28 pricing was 59% on overnight index swaps as of September 25, a coin flip, with most bank economists expecting a hold through 2026. Against CAD: August employment -41.7k after +75.1k, the services and composite PMIs at 46.8 and 47.8 are the only contracting readings on this board, wage growth 3.2% and falling, unemployment 6.4%. For it: headline CPI 3.0% with core rising to 2.4% from 2.3%, August retail sales +1.3% m/m, Q2 GDP 0.8% q/q and 1.1% y/y, manufacturing PMI 53.0, PPI 12.4%. USD/CAD 1.41982, +0.06% today, +0.68% on the week, +2.48% on the month. Canada 10-year 3.9670.",
      forward: "🔻 Dovish into October 28, DOWN from +1 to -1, and the two-point cut needs justifying because yesterday's stated test only specified one point. The test was explicit: monthly GDP today at 12:30Z, forecast cut to 0.0% from 0.3%, and a flat or negative print reasserts the domestic story, takes the coin flip toward a hold and would justify cutting CAD to 0. It printed 0.0%, so that cut is earned on the page's own terms. The second point comes from the leg the test did not cover, because yesterday the +1 was explicitly said to rest on the inflation-and-terms-of-trade legs rather than the pricing leg - and it is the terms-of-trade leg that broke today. Middle East exports are back to 98% of pre-war levels, WTI is 89.627, and Macklem's own conditional for inflation edging up was oil staying near 100 dollars. So the domestic leg failed a dated test and the commodity leg failed underneath it, on the same morning, which is a genuine two-point move rather than momentum. The ratePaths entry flips from hike to hold accordingly, and note that this puts the page back onside with the economists: 59% on OIS was always a coin flip and RBC among others expected no move in 2026. There is an internal tension this page flagged yesterday that now resolves, and resolving it cleanly is worth more than the score. The words were: a USOIL LONG alongside a USD/CAD LONG is coherent only because the barrel is not reaching the currency, and if that changes both positions need revisiting rather than one. The barrel changed - downward - so USOIL flips to SHORT and USD/CAD stays LONG, which makes the pair of positions coherent in the same direction for the first time this week instead of merely tolerable. The verdict moves to WEAKENING because there is now nothing left holding the currency up except a core CPI at 2.4% and a 12.4% PPI that the Bank has already said it will look through. What invalidates -1: a Gulf re-escalation that takes WTI back above 100, which restores Macklem's conditional verbatim and is the single cleanest reversal; the August GDP revision coming in well above +0.2%; or a hawkish Monetary Policy Report on October 28 that treats tariffs rather than oil as the inflation driver. The Business Outlook Survey on October 19 is the earlier read. Canada is on a bank holiday today, so the next domestic input is the manufacturing PMI tomorrow at 13:30Z with Rogers speaking at 19:05Z."
