      tag: "Zero until December 10, the haven bid has now failed three tests, and a normalising supply picture removes the escalation that was its last hope",
      drivers: "TE re-verified: the SNB policy rate is 0.00%, held in September for a fifth consecutive meeting, next assessed December 10. TE's summary: the stance remains appropriate, inflation has risen to 0.8% in August driven mainly by higher energy prices, the SNB expects it to rise further in Q4 before easing through 2027, and forecasts average inflation of 0.7% in 2026 and 0.8% in both 2027 and 2028, with GDP growth of 1.5-2% in 2026. The September 24 assessment softened the intervention language to being willing to act in the FX market only 'as necessary', which Societe Generale read as the intervention bias being dialled down, and Schlegel has closed the downside by citing the undesirable side effects of negative rates. USD/CHF 0.83466, +0.09% today, +1.14% on the week, +3.25% on the month, +5.27% YTD - at fresh multi-year highs made on two consecutive escalation days with crude up 1.83% on each, while gold printed an eight-week low. Rate and yield disadvantage: 400bp behind the Fed, 460bp behind the RBA, 375bp behind Bank Rate, 250bp behind the ECB deposit rate and 125bp behind even the BoJ, with a 10-year at 0.6450. Inflation 0.8% in August from 0.4%, core just 0.4%, harmonised 0.9%, wage growth collapsed to 0.4% from 1.8% - the clearest no-second-round-effects reading on the board. Activity is strong and irrelevant to the currency: Q2 GDP 1.5% q/q and 2.3% y/y, manufacturing PMI 57.1 from 53.2, services 58.3, unemployment 3.0%, PPI -0.7%. CPI tomorrow at 06:30Z is forecast at 0.0% m/m against 0.4%.",
      forward: "🔻 Dovish and unchanged at -2, and it stays the highest-conviction structural read here because the thesis has now survived three different tests rather than been re-argued three times. A short franc during a Middle East war is a contrarian position, and it has been handed its two ideal counter-tests on consecutive sessions - Trump's rejection of the Iranian proposal, then reporting that Tehran doubts a deal before the November midterms, both with crude up 1.83% - and on both USD/CHF made fresh multi-year highs while gold fell to an eight-week low. The third test is today's, and it runs the other way: the supply picture is normalising, exports are back to 98% of pre-war levels, so the escalation that would have been required to finally produce a haven bid is itself now less likely. A position that wins on escalation not arriving and on escalation arriving is the definition of the right side of a trade. There is a specific new point about Swiss inflation worth making, because it is the only channel by which the SNB could ever have moved: the entire rise to 0.8% was energy, with core at 0.4% and wages at 0.4%. The Bank's own forecast expects inflation to rise further in Q4 before easing, and that Q4 pickup was an energy forecast. A lower barrel points it back toward the bottom of a range that already runs 0.7% in 2026 and 0.8% in 2027 and 2028 - so there was no case for a hike, and now there is even less of one, while Schlegel's objection to negative rates closes the other side. Zero for five meetings becomes zero for eight with nothing in the Bank's own two-year forecast requiring a move. Two events tomorrow can touch it. CPI at 06:30Z, forecast 0.0% m/m against 0.4%, where a flat or negative print confirms there is no domestic inflation to respond to at all. Then Schlegel at 15:30Z, where the only thing that matters is whether he hardens back toward intervention - and with the franc depreciating he has less reason to than at any point this year. The Quarterly Bulletin lands today at 13:00Z and Tschudin speaks at 14:30Z. What invalidates -2: a genuine military escalation that closes the strait harder and finally produces the bid three sessions have refused, which would hurt USD/CHF, GBP/CHF, AUD/CHF and CAD/CHF simultaneously; or a US payroll miss on Friday large enough to collapse the rate gap from the dollar side, which hurts USD/CHF alone and leaves the crosses intact. CHF/JPY SHORT at 188.281 is now the purest expression on this page - a five-point score gap between the two zero-to-low yielders, one of which has 4.7% wage growth and a hiking central bank."
